                          <a href=search.php?query=&topic=33&author=><img src=images/topics/raids.gif border=0 Alt="Guild Raids" align=right hspace=10 vspace=10></a>                          Magg heard his friend Noyr’s death gurgle and knew that his time had come.  He watched with astonishment as the great beast leapt effortlessly over the fallen shadow knight’s corpse, and felt the searing pain of the kobold’s claws as they ripped through his armor as if it were paper.  Magg had survived many battles on the strength of his matchless sword play and bravery, but against the savage fury of his attacker he could do little but delay the killing blow.  (read on . . . )<br><br><br><br>He fell back, step by step as the kobold ripped away his life spirit.  Yet even among the despair of his fallen comrades, there came a flicker of hope.  Magg could see that the white-furred kobold was bleeding profusely from several gaping wounds.  If he could just buy his remaining comrades some time, perhaps they could still overcome their brutal enemy.
“Mithaniel Marr!  Hear me now in my hour of need!”  Magg screamed his plea to the heavens, and in return he felt the familiar pulse of life, of energy, returning to his broken limbs.  With an exultant cry he stopped his retreat and thrust a swift counter-strike under his opponent’s blow.  He felt the satisfying shock as his prized blade found some internal organ of the beast, and felt the wrench as the kobold flinched away from his strike.  But still the blow of the mighty kobold leader fell on his unguarded neck, and as Magg fell into the blackness of eternal slumber, he could see, to his horror, that his opponent still stood and still fought.
Khelkar Icepaw had ruled the underground caverns of The Labyrinth for some 22 years, and in all that time no adventuring party from the surface had ever lived to leave and spread the tale of the horrors that lurked underneath the Castle of the great Sorceror, Velketor.  Now he could sense that another victory was imminent, for although this group of treasure-seekers had entered his lair with a well-prepared strategy, and had slaughtered many of his best, there remained but a few poor wretches to test his might.  He paused for a moment over the prone form of  the brave paladin, and uttered an awful cry of rage and impending doom.
That cry was cut short by a mighty blow from a great axe, landing squarely between Khelkar’s shoulder blades.  The mighty Khelkar spun around and saw his foe, and laughed.  It was but a stripling of a fighter, a troll, but unseasoned and unscarred by the test of battles.  When this troll lay dead, Khelkar would dispose of the few remaining magic users huddled in a corner of his lair.
Czrak did not laugh.  He stood among the slaughter of his dead comrades, bodies strewn helter-skelter across the floor of the cave, testament to the might of Khelkar, but he could see that the kobold was grievously hurt.  With a silent prayer to the God of War, Czrak hurled himself upon his foe, but even with the giant kobold badly hurt, he might not have carried the day were it not for the intervention of his friend Kirya.  The elven cleric had seen her husband Jaysonn die in the initial onslaught, but despite the grief which racked her body she stood her ground, and even now was able to cast the intricate spells which healed the troll Czrak and spelled defeat for Khelkar.  The kobold fell back unbelieving as the troll, near death, suddenly recovered and stuck him again with the blackened axe.  A wound opened up in the kobold’s neck, and more of his precious lifeblood spilled out.  Too much.  He had lost too much, expended too much energy, and now, with just one warrior remaining, it was too late.  He could fight no more.  His ferocious eyes glazed over the troll smote him a final time.
Czrak was victorious.  The party had survived.  But at what a cost!  Just 5 remained of the initial 19 who had dropped down the icy pit into the lair of the kobolds.  Never was a victory bought with such a bitter taste of defeat.
 
 

